  "dataSource": {
    "url": "https://assets.ctfassets.net/0tc4847zqy12/bQFJBCmsAk4ww4qzdfAOm/27c88d608796913d6a47899ecffb6a3c/Nutrition_8.5.26.pdf",
    "fetchedAt": "2026-08-07",
    "fetchedBy": "research agent, verified against the official source",
    "notes": "SOURCE: Qdoba's own \"Nutrition Facts 2026\" PDF (file Nutrition_8.5.26.pdf, i.e. revised 2026-08-05 — current as of retrieval), linked as the \"NUTRITION\" download from https://www.qdoba.com/nutrition-allergens. The PDF is served from Qdoba's Contentful CDN (assets.ctfassets.net), which is why sourceUrl is a ctfassets.net host rather than qdoba.com — it is still Qdoba's own published document, reached only by following the link on their official nutrition page. www.qdoba.com itself is behind Cloudflare and returns 403 to non-browser fetches; I loaded the nutrition page in a real browser to obtain the current PDF link, then downloaded and text-extracted the PDF directly (pypdf).\n\nPORTION BASIS: Every number is per one of the stated serving, exactly as the PDF lists it — no scaling, no reverse-engineering, no derived values anywhere in this menu. The PDF's column order is: Allergens, Serving Size (g), Calories, Calories from Fat, Total Fat, Saturated Fat, Trans Fat, Cholesterol, Total Carbohydrate, Dietary Fiber, Sugar, Protein, Sodium, Potassium. I mapped Total Fat / Total Carbohydrate / Protein and validated the mapping by Atwater cross-check (cal ~= 4C + 4P + 9F) on ~35 rows; all reconciled within normal rounding/fiber tolerance except the items called out below.\n\nWHERE THE SOURCE LISTS TWO SIZES, I kept one canonical unit and let the app's portion mechanics cover the other: guacamole, Three Cheese Queso and Queso Diablo use the 2 oz scoop (the 4 oz rows are exact 2x, and `dips` caps at 3 scoops); chorizo uses 3.0 oz and bacon 2 oz (the 1.5 oz / 1 oz rows are exact halves, covered by the split-base half-portion rule).\n\nKNOWN SOURCE DEFECTS, reported as published and flagged rather than silently corrected:\n1. Salsa Verde (1 oz) is published with Total Carbohydrate 0 g but Dietary Fiber 1 g — internally impossible. I report carbs = 0 as published. Impact is negligible (10 cal item), but do not treat that 0 as verified.\n2. Seasoned Potatoes (2 oz) is published as 100 cal with 12 C / 1 P / 0 F; those macros only account for ~52 cal. Their own \"Calories from Fat\" column is also 0, so there is no fat figure to recover. Reported as published; the fat value is likely understated.\n3. Brisket Birria (3.5 oz) has \"Calories from Fat\" printed as 140, equal to its total calories — a typo — but its Total Fat of 7 g is consistent with the 140 cal total, so the macros I use are sound.\n\nSIGNATURE EATS AS ENTREES: The `entrees` category holds Qdoba's published complete named entrees at their exact published totals. These are whole meals, not counter stations — they are included because the macros are fully official and directly loggable. They should NOT be combined with build-your-own components (that would double count).\n\nPRESETS ARE INTENTIONALLY EMPTY — see the reasoning: Qdoba publishes the TOTALS for its Signature Eats but nowhere publishes the component/portion breakdown of any of them. I attempted reconciliation and it does not converge. Example: Keto Bowl - Chicken (published 340 g / 400 cal / 26 F / 11 C / 33 P); chicken + shredded cheese + guac + sour cream sums to 212 g / 430 cal / 34 F / 28 P — wrong on weight, fat and protein. Example: Chicken Queso Bowl (published 468 g / 750 cal / 33 F / 74 C / 43 P); the closest plausible build (rice + black beans + chicken + 4 oz queso + pico) gives 695 cal and no arrangement of published line items lands on 750/33/74/43. The signature builds evidently use in-house portions that differ from the standard line-item servings. Double Protein Bowl - Chicken is the one partial exception — its published cholesterol of 180 mg is exactly 2x the 90 mg of a 3.5 oz chicken portion, confirming double chicken — but the remainder still misses by ~10 cal / 2 g fat / 2 g protein, which is a real defect for a macro app. Rather than ship a component list Qdoba never published and that fails the sum check, I returned zero presets, per the brief's explicit allowance. If presets are wanted later, the honest path is Qdoba's in-app ordering builder, not this PDF."
  },
